Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

I have this problem when setting second account for remote access via RStudio server. Here is my current settings:
I have a super user A, and a hadoop user B.

On HDFS, I have
drwxr-xr-x   - A supergroup          0 2014-04-09 14:27 /tmp
drwxr-xr-x   - A supergroup          0 2014-04-09 15:29 /user
drwxr-xr-x   - B hadoop              0 2014-04-09 15:29 /user/B
drwxr-xr-x   - A supergroup          0 2013-12-10 11:16 /user/A
drwxr-xr-x   - B hadoop              0 2014-04-09 15:29 /user/B/tmp

When I login to RStudio Server as B, I have this:
> tempdir()
[1] "/tmp/RtmpY3yVBq"

On my local file system, you can see this directory owner is B.
drwx------ 2 B   B        4096 Apr  9 15:41 RtmpY3yVBq

Also, I have this settings hadoop.tmp.dir=/tmp/hadoop, not /tmp/hadoop-{user-name}, and it's owned by user A group hadoop.
drwxr-xr-x 3 A   hadoop    4096 Apr  9 15:41 hadoop

The problem is, I'm able to ls and mkdir, which means my current user B have access to his user directory.
> hdfs.mkdir('/user/B/test')
[1] TRUE

But when I run to.dfs, it gives me Permission Denied error:
> small.ints = to.dfs(1:1000)
mkdir: org.apache.hadoop.security.AccessControlException: Permission denied: user=B, access=WRITE, inode="tmp":A:supergroup:rwxr-xr-x
Error: dfs.mkdir(dfs.tempdir) is not TRUE
In addition: Warning message:
In to.dfs(1:1000) : Converting to.dfs argument to keyval with a NULL key

All right, it's trying to write to /tmp in HDFS, which won't work. So I changed dfs.tempdir to user B's tmp directory, but still get permission denied.
> rmr.options(dfs.tempdir = '/user/B/tmp')
NULL
> small.ints = to.dfs(1:1000)
Exception in thread "main" java.io.IOException: Permission denied
	at java.io.UnixFileSystem.createFileExclusively(Native Method)
	at java.io.File.createNewFile(File.java:1006)
	at java.io.File.createTempFile(File.java:1989)
	at org.apache.hadoop.util.RunJar.main(RunJar.java:119)
Warning message:
In to.dfs(1:1000) : Converting to.dfs argument to keyval with a NULL key

In command line, I'm able to run -copyFromLocal fine, which means user B does have WRITE access to it's user directory.

So I'm confused. What am I missing here?

Thanks in advance for any help!

-Lishu

